\section{Introduction (with Literature Review)}

\subsection{Background}

Randomized Controlled Trials (RCTs) remain the gold standard for evaluating the efficacy and safety of novel therapeutic interventions. 
However, the clinical development sector is increasingly constrained by raising operational costs, strict time limitations, and complex 
ethical considerations \cite{dimasi_innovation_2016}. For instance, recruiting a sufficient number of patients, particularly in case of 
rare diseases, poses a major obstacle to the development of new treatments. Furthermore, randomizing patients to a placebo or active 
control arm when other effective treatments already exist raises significant ethical concerns regarding patient welfare \cite{freedman_scientific_1987, world_medical_association_world_2025}.

Historically, the dominant paradigm in the design of RCTs has been the frequentist approach. This framework evaluates long-run operating 
characteristics, relying heavily on strict Type I error control. A fundamental limitation of this paradigm is that it treats every trial 
in isolation, ignoring pre-existing informations such as data from previous clinical phases or historical control arms that could otherwise 
help the current study. 

Conversely, Bayesian statistical frameworks offer a mathematically rigorous solution to this challenge by incorporating external evidence 
through informative prior distributions. Integrating background knowledge during the planning phase can lead to highly efficient designs, 
accelerating trial execution, reducing operational waste, and limiting the number of concurrent patients required for the control arm (\textcolor{red}{Citations of examples?}). 
This not only decreases the overall sample size but also makes the trial more attractive to potential participants, who face a lower 
probability of being assigned to a placebo group. As noted by O'Hagan et al., \textit{"the design of a trial 
is ultimately the sponsor’s decision and its outcome is the sponsor’s risk; therefore, the design should incorporate all available information to 
maximize the probability of a satisfactory outcome"} \cite{ohagan_assurance_2005}.
Recognizing these practical benefits, regulatory bodies are increasingly supportive of these methods, as 
evidenced by recent guidance on the use of Bayesian methodology in clinical trials \cite{noauthor_use_nodate}.



\subsection{Bayesian Designs and Operating Characteristics}
The Bayesian framework formally incorporates uncertainty into the design through prior distributions, that can be placed either directly 
on the treatment effect or on one (or both) arms:

\begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Prior on the Control Group:} when recruiting patients for a standard-of-care or placebo arm is ethically challenging or 
    operationally slow, historical data can be used to inform the control parameters. Designs that supplement a smaller concurrent control group 
    with historical information are formally referred to in the literature as hybrid-control trials. \textcolor{red}{Weru cites a lot of hybrid control trials.} 


    \item \textbf{Prior on the Treatment Effect:} When the primary goal is to use existing efficacy data to support a new indication or 
    demographic, such as leveraging established adult data to pediatric cohorts, the prior is typically constructed to inform the active treatment 
    parameter or the treatment contrast directly \cite{psioda_bayesian_2019}.
\end{itemize}




Evaluating the operating characteristics of Bayesian designs requires particular attention. Traditional frequentist designs rely on 
conditional power, estimating the probability of success assuming a single, fixed treatment effect. The Bayesian framework shifts instead the focus 
to unconditional power, or Assurance, which evaluates the expected power averaged over a prior distribution for the unknown treatment effect.
Integrating over a prior provides a much more realistic assessment of the predictive probability of achieving a 
statistically significant result \cite{spiegelhalter1993bayesian, ohagan_assurance_2005}.

Despite these fundamentally different philosophies regarding power, it remains common practice to evaluate Bayesian designs with informative priors 
using the classical, frequentist Type I error rate. However, when Type I error is defined traditionally, conditioning solely on the sampling distribution 
of the current trial's data, it is mathematically impossible to strictly control it if external information is actively being borrowed 
\cite{viele_use_2014, psioda_bayesian_2019, kopp-schneider_power_2020}. 

Therefore, evaluating a Bayesian design requires a distinctly Bayesian error metric, one that possesses the same essential property for 
Bayesian designs that the classical Type I error holds for frequentist designs, namely, that it can be rigorously controlled at a pre-specified level. This 
goal is obtained through the Average Type I Error\cite{best_beyond_2025}, a metric that marginalizes the false positive 
risk over a specified design prior (in contrast to the analysis prior, obtained by historical data), providing a more appropriate evaluation of Bayesian 
operating characteristics. \textcolor{red}{Also talk abou tthe other metric in case of prior on trt }

\subsection{Information Borrowing and Prior-Data Conflict}

While borrowing historical data presents clear efficiency gains, it is not without risk. It is possible that historical data and current trial 
data are fundamentally heterogeneous due to shifts in demographics, standard of care, or measurement techniques \cite{evans_checking_2006}. If a model 
forces the integration of historical and concurrent data when such a discrepancy exists, it can lead to severely biased estimates and inflated error rates. 
Indeed, the naive pooling of historical data leads to unacceptable Type I error inflation under prior-data 
conflict, firmly establishing the necessity for dynamic borrowing techniques \cite{viele_use_2014}.

\textcolor{red}{Schmidli 2015 has a nice lit review}
Recognizing these dangers, early frequentist guidelines emphasized strict criteria to ensure external data were 
sufficiently comparable before any pooling \cite{pocock_combination_1976}. \textcolor{red}{NOT SURE ABOUT THIS} While traditional frequentist approaches often relied on rigid "test-and-pool" 
methods, modern Bayesian frameworks address the risk of prior-data conflict through dynamic borrowing. Rather than blindly pooling 
or entirely discarding historical data, dynamic borrowing algorithms assess the congruency between the historical evidence and the accumulating new data.
 If the historical data aligns well with the current trial, the model "borrows" strength from it, thereby increasing precision. Conversely, if there is a 
 substantial true difference between the historical and current parameters, commonly referred to as "drift", the model dynamically discounts the historical 
 information, relying more heavily on the concurrent data.

Several approaches have been proposed to achieve this dynamic behavior. Examples include Power Priors \cite{ibrahim_power_2000}, 
Commensurate Priors \cite{hobbs_2011}, and Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) priors \cite{noauthor_summarizing_nodate}. This thesis will focus
 primarily on the MAP framework, and specifically on its robust extension. By summarizing historical information into a predictive prior, MAP priors allow 
 for structured borrowing \cite{noauthor_summarizing_nodate}. Because standard MAP priors can sometimes be too slow to react to severe prior-data conflict, 
 \textit{Robust Mixture Priors} jave been proposed \cite{schmidli_robust_2014}. By blending an informative MAP component with a diffuse robust component, 
 these priors provide a highly flexible mechanism that safely adapts to varying degrees of drift, ensuring robust inference even under severe prior 
 misspecification \cite{schmidli_robust_2014}. \textcolor{red}{"Information Borrowing in Bayesian Clinical Trials: choice of Tuning Parameters for the Robust Mixture Prior" has other citations in the intro}

 
