\subsection{Classic and Bayesian Fixed-Design Metrics}


Frequentist Type I error and power are well-established metrics used to evaluate traditional frequentist trial designs. However, it is documented that when informative priors are incorporated into a 
design, strict point-wise control of the frequentist Type I error is impossible \cite{viele_use_2014, kopp-schneider_power_2020}. Consequently, evaluating Bayesian designs requires alternative metrics, such 
as Assurance \cite{ohagan_assurance_2005} or Predictive Power \cite{micheloud_power_2022, spiegelhalter_monitoring_1986}.

As noted by \cite{best_beyond_2025}, all these metrics are special cases of a common metric $m$ which can be expressed as the expected probability of study success, integrated over a 
specified prior distribution for the parameter of interest $\theta$:
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:m}
m (CP(\delta), p(\delta)) = \int \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta\right) p(\theta) \, d\theta
\end{equation}

Which is simply the expected value of the conditional power with respect to the design prior, $E_{p(\theta)}[CP(\theta)]$.

If $p(\theta)$ is defined as a Dirac delta function at a specific point estimate $\theta^*$ (i.e., assuming the parameter is fixed and known), Equation~\ref{eq:m} collapses into the classical 
frequentist conditional power (or classical point-wise Type I error if evaluated under the null hypothesis). Relying on this conditional formulation ignores the inherent 
uncertainty of the underlying parameter, this is why $p(\theta)$ can be specified as a continuous probability distribution representing the uncertainty surrounding the true parameter. In this case,
Equation~\ref{eq:m} yields the predictive power, often referred to as \textit{Assurance} \cite{ohagan_assurance_2005}, probability of study success 
\cite{wang_evaluating_2013}, or Bayesian predictive power \cite{spiegelhalter_predictive_1986}. This specification allows us to obtain a more conservative value, since the power
obtained with predictive methods is always closer to 50\% than the power obtained with conditional methods \textcolor{red}{(Spiegelhalter, Abrams and Myles, 2004, Grouin et al.,
2007, Dallow and Fina, 2011)}.




We differentiate between design and analysis prior \cite{ohagan_bayesian_2001}.  
The analysis prior is the distribution used in the actual analysis to derive the posterior (e.g., an informative prior obtained via a Meta-Analytic-Predictive approach). Conversely, 
the design prior $p(\theta)$, which appears in formula \ref{eq:m}, represents the assumed distribution of the underlying true parameter used to evaluate the design's operating characteristics.

When evaluating these operating characteristics, using the exact same prior beliefs for both the data generation (design) and the inference (analysis) is known as calculating the 
fully Bayesian power \cite{micheloud_power_2022}. Utilizing the same prior in both stages is considered the most logically consistent and natural approach by many Bayesian 
methodologists \cite{ohagan_bayesian_2001}.
However, the differentiation of design and analysis prior allows for sensitivity analysis in order to calibrate the design. This allows to understand the behavior of a Bayesian design under 
"drift" scenarios where the prior information used for analysis differs from the true data generating process, practice that has been advocated by sevewal authors (Wang and Gelfand 2002; Spiegelhalter, Abrams, and Myles 2004)



\subsubsection{Prior on control}
When analysis prior information is available on the control $\theta_2$ and/or $\theta_1$, we can modify Equation~\ref{eq:m} toa account for design priors $p(\theta_2)$ and/or $p(\theta_1)$:
$$m (CP(\theta_1, \theta2), p(\theta_1, \theta_2)) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_1, \theta_2\right) p(\theta_1) p(\theta_2) \, d\theta_1 \, d\theta_2$$


The traditional definitions of both Type I Error and power impose deterministic constraints 
on the relationship between the treatment and control parameters. Specifically, we can fix the treatment 
contrast at a constant value $\delta^*$, such that $\theta_1 = \theta_2 + \delta^*$. In this framework,
$\delta^*$ represents the null effect (e.g., $\delta^* = 0$) when evaluating Type I Error, or the 
targeted alternative effect when evaluating power. Therefore, a design prior is only required for one of these parameters to evaluate the design.

\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:avgT1E}
  m = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_2, \theta_1 = \theta_2 + \delta^*\right) p(\theta_2) \, d\theta_2
\end{equation}

When $\delta^* = 0$, we refer to Equation~\eqref{eq:avgT1E} as \textbf{Average Type I Error}:  
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:avg-type1-error}
  \alpha_{avg} = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_2, \theta_1 = \theta_2\right) p(\theta_2) \, d\theta_2
\end{equation}


Note that in this case, the classical T1E, obtaining by defining $p(\theta_2)$ as a Dirac in $\theta_2$, is no longer a single constant, instead it becomes a pointwise rate that varies as a function of $\theta_2$. 
It has been proved the if the specified design prior coincides with the analysis prior, then the design achieves strict asymptotic control of $\alpha_{avg}$ at the nominal level \cite{best_beyond_2025}. 

As an alternative, it is also proposed to compute the maximum (or minimum) operating characteristic over a plausible subset of the parameter space, 
denoted as $\Theta_2$. Then this restricted maximum Type I error is expressed as:
$$\max_{\theta_2 \in \Theta_2} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_2, \theta_1 = \theta_2 + \delta^*\right)$$



\paragraph{Computational Framework}
Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ denote the sufficient statistics, (in this case the sample means Since as mentioned in Intro, we are working with normally distributed endpoints) for the treatment 
and control arms, respectively. Then assume wlog that lower values are better

Success is determined by a decision function $D(y_1, y_2) \in \{0, 1\}$, which evaluates whether the posterior distribution of the treatment contrast, 
$\Delta = \theta_1 - \theta_2$, satisfies a set of predefined conditions. For instance, a standard design requires the posterior probability to 
exceed a specific treshold: $\Pr(\theta_1 - \theta_2 \le q_c \mid y_1, y_2) > p_c$ for specific critical values $q_c$ and probabilities $p_c$.

When evaluating multiple conditions simultaneously, such as in dual-criterion designs requiring both statistical significance and clinical relevance 
\cite{roychoudhury_beyond_2018}, the decision is formulated as the product of Heaviside step functions for each condition\footnote{Note that this product 
formulation naturally generalizes to an arbitrary number of conditions} $H(x)$, where $H(x) = 1$ for $x > 0$ and $0$ for $x \le 0$:

$$D(y_1, y_2) = \prod_{i} H\Big(\Pr(\theta_1 - \theta_2 \le q_{c,i} \mid y_1, y_2) - p_{c,i}\Big)$$ 



The specification of priors, the sample sisez ($n_1$, $n_2$), and the decision function $D(y_1, y_2)$ uniquely define a conditional Decision Boundary 
$D_1(y_2)$ for each observed control outcome $y_2$,
$$D_1(y_2) = \max\{y_1 : D(y_1, y_2) = 1\}$$ 
This represents the maximal threshold the treatment data $y_1$ must satisfy to declare success.

Given this boundary, we can compute the conditional probability of success $\Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_1, \theta_2)$ required in Equation \ref{eq:m}. Assuming
 true parameters $\theta_1$ and $\theta_2$, the overall probability of a "Go" decision is the expectation of crossing the boundary $D_1(y_2)$ over the sampling 
 distribution of the control data:
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:oc2S_general}
  \Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_1, \theta_2) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} F_1\left(D_1(y_2) \mid \theta_1\right) f_2\left(y_2 \mid \theta_2\right) \, dy_2
\end{equation}
where $F_1\left(\cdot \mid \theta_1\right)$ is the cumulative distribution function of the treatment statistic evaluated at the decision boundary, and
 $f_2\left(\cdot \mid \theta_2\right)$ is the probability density function of the control statistic.

Because we are working with normally distributed endpoints, the sampling distributions for the sample means are normal: 
$y_k \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_k, SEM_k^2)$ for $k \in \{1, 2\}$, where $SEM_k = \sigma_k / \sqrt{n_k}$. Consequently, the general integral in 
Equation \ref{eq:oc2S_general} can be evaluated directly using the standard normal CDF ($\Phi$) and PDF ($\phi$):
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:oc2S_normal}
  \Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_1, \theta_2) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Phi\left(\frac{D_1(y_2) - \theta_1}{SEM_1}\right) \frac{1}{SEM_2} \phi\left(\frac{y_2 - \theta_2}{SEM_2}\right) \, dy_2
\end{equation}
 
