\subsection{Extending to Sequential Bayesian Metrics}
The concepts established for fixed-sample designs naturally extend to group sequential designs. In a GSD, the data are evaluated at $K$ interim 
looks. Let $n_{1,k}$ and $n_{2,k}$ denote the cumulative sample sizes at stage $k \in \{1, \dots, K\}$, and let $y_{1,k}$ and $y_{2,k}$ represent the 
corresponding sufficient statistics (sample means).

At each interim look $k$, the decision to stop for efficacy, stop for futility, or continue to the next stage is dictated by the posterior distribution 
of the treatment contrast $\Delta = \theta_1 - \theta_2$. To evaluate these sequential operating characteristics, we must define the distribution of the 
data across the interim looks. Because the cumulative sample sizes monotonically increase ($n_{m,1} < n_{m,2} < \dots < n_{m,K}$ for arm $m \in \{1, 2\}$), 
the data at any stage perfectly contains all information from the previous stages. 

\paragraph{Stage 1 (Marginal Distribution)}
At the first interim analysis, the observed sample mean depends entirely on the true underlying effect $\theta_m$ and the initial sample size $n_{m,1}$. 
The standard error is simply $SEM_{m,1} = \sigma_m / \sqrt{n_{m,1}}$. Therefore, the initial cumulative mean follows a normal distribution centered on the 
true effect:$$y_{m,1} \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_m, SEM_{m,1}^2)$$

\paragraph{Subsequent Stages (Conditional Distributions)}If the trial continues to a subsequent stage $k$ (where $1 < k \le K$), the new cumulative mean 
$y_{m,k}$ is not entirely random; it is anchored by the data already observed at the previous stage, $y_{m,k-1}$. The final cumulative mean is a
weighted average of the known interim data and the data from the new cohort of patients, denoted $\Delta n_{m,k} = n_{m,k} - n_{m,k-1}$.
Given the prior interim result $y_{m,k-1}$, the expected value of the updated mean pulls the observed data toward the true underlying effect $\theta_m$:
$$\mathbb{E}[y_{m,k} \mid y_{m,k-1}] = \frac{n_{m,k-1}y_{m,k-1} + \Delta n_{m,k}\theta_m}{n_{m,k}}$$

Since the variance of the fixed historical data $y_{m,k-1}$ is zero, the variance of the new cumulative mean derives entirely from the new patients, 
shrinking proportionally to the contribution of the new cohort: 
$$\mathbb{V}\text{ar}(y_{m,k} \mid y_{m,k-1}) = \frac{\sigma_m^2 \Delta n_{m,k}}{n_{m,k}^2}$$

Consequently, the data at any stage $k$, conditional on the result of the preceding stage, follows a shifted and narrowed normal transition density:

$$f_{m, k|k-1}(y_{m,k}) \sim \mathcal{N}\left( \mathbb{E}[y_{m,k}] , \mathbb{V}\text{ar}(y_{m,k}) \right)$$


