\subsubsection{Sequential Decision Boundaries and Regions}
Similar to the fixed-design case, the Bayesian decision functions evaluated at each look $k$ uniquely define conditional boundaries on the data scale. 
Assuming wlog that lower values indicate efficacy, we define two critical boundaries for the treatment mean given the observed control mean 
$y_{2,k}$:\begin{itemize}\item \textbf{Efficacy Boundary} $D_{1,k}(y_{2,k})$: The maximal threshold $y_{1,k}$ must satisfy to declare early or final 
study success.\item \textbf{Futility Boundary} $F_{1,k}(y_{2,k})$: The minimal threshold below which $y_{1,k}$ must fall to justify continuing the trial; 
otherwise, the trial stops for futility.\end{itemize}

These boundaries partition the bivariate sample space at stage $k$ into three mutually exclusive regions:
\begin{align*}\text{Efficacy Region:} \quad &\mathcal{R}k = { (y{1,k}, y_{2,k}) : y_{1,k} \le D_{1,k}(y_{2,k}) } \\text{Futility Region:} \quad &\mathcal{F}k = { (y{1,k}, y_{2,k}) : y_{1,k} > F_{1,k}(y_{2,k}) } \\text{Continuation Region:} \quad &\mathcal{C}_k = \left( \mathcal{R}_k \cup \mathcal{F}_k \right)^c\end{align*}Note that at the final stage $K$, the continuation region is empty ($\mathcal{C}_K = \emptyset$), forcing a terminal decision.


\subsubsection{Sequential Conditional and Average Operating Characteristics}
In the sequential framework, the conditional probability of success $\Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_1, \theta_2)$ from Equation~\ref{eq:m} is the probability of the trial trajectory entering an efficacy region $\mathcal{R}_k$ at any stage $k$, conditional on having survived the continuation regions $\mathcal{C}_j$ at all previous stages $j < k$.This pointwise sequential operating characteristic is expressed as a sum of joint probabilities:\begin{equation}\label{eq:seq_conditional_success}\Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_1, \theta_2) = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \Pr\left( (y_{1,k}, y_{2,k}) \in \mathcal{R}k ;\bigcap{j=1}^{k-1}; (y_{1,j}, y_{2,j}) \in \mathcal{C}_j \Big| \theta_1, \theta_2 \right)\end{equation}When evaluated under the null hypothesis constraint ($\theta_1 = \theta_2 + \delta^*$ where $\delta^* = 0$), Equation~\ref{eq:seq_conditional_success} yields the conditional sequential Type I error, $T1E(\theta_2)$.Because the efficacy and futility boundaries $D_{1,k}(\cdot)$ and $F_{1,k}(\cdot)$ dynamically shift based on the degree of conflict between the interim control data $y_{2,k}$ and the analysis prior $p(\theta_2)$, $T1E(\theta_2)$ is highly sensitive to the true value of $\theta_2$. To obtain a robust design metric, we marginalize this conditional sequential error over the design prior $p(\theta_2)$, defining the \textbf{Sequential Average Type I Error}:\begin{equation}\label{eq:seq_avg_t1e}\alpha_{avg, seq} = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \left[ \sum_{k=1}^{K} \Pr\left( (y_{1,k}, y_{2,k}) \in \mathcal{R}k ;\bigcap{j=1}^{k-1}; (y_{1,j}, y_{2,j}) \in \mathcal{C}_j \Big| \theta_2, \theta_1=\theta_2 \right) \right] p(\theta_2) , d\theta_2\end{equation}As in the fixed design, the alignment of the design prior with the analysis prior governs the behavior of the Average Type I Error. However, the exact asymptotic control achieved in fixed designs does not directly translate to the group sequential setting. Because the data are monitored at discrete interim looks, the sequential trajectory of the posterior probability forms a martingale under the prior predictive distribution. By Doob's maximal inequality, if the design prior perfectly matches the analysis prior, evaluating a constant posterior probability threshold $p_c$ across all interim looks guarantees a conservative upper bound on the Sequential Average Type I Error: $\alpha_{avg, seq} \le 1 - p_c$.Strict equality (exact control) is generally not achieved due to boundary overshoot; the discrete accumulation of data causes the posterior probability to jump past the threshold rather than land exactly on it. Consequently, achieving a precise target level for $\alpha_{avg, seq}$ in a Bayesian sequential design requires actively calibrating the boundaries $D_{1,k}(\cdot)$, for instance by numerically tuning the posterior thresholds $p_c$ to account for the sequential overshoot.\paragraph{Computational Framework for Sequential Metrics}In a fixed design, Equation~\ref{eq:oc2S_normal} allows for rapid computation of $\alpha_{avg}$ via single or double numerical quadrature. However, the sequential equation (Equation~\ref{eq:seq_avg_t1e}) requires evaluating the integral of a $2K$-dimensional multivariate normal density over complex, non-linear boundaries defined by mixture analysis priors.For $K \ge 2$, direct numerical integration becomes computationally prohibitive. Instead, $\alpha_{avg, seq}$ is efficiently evaluated using Monte Carlo integration tailored to the properties of cumulative sufficient statistics. The algorithm proceeds by defining the boundaries $\mathcal{R}_k$ and $\mathcal{F}_k$ exclusively on the data scale \textit{a priori}, stripping the computationally expensive Bayesian mixture updating out of the simulation loop.By drawing $S$ independent samples of the true control parameter from the design prior, $\theta_2^{(s)} \sim p(\theta_2)$, we simulate the incremental sequence of data. The incremental mean for stage $k$ depends only on the new patients $\Delta n_{m,k} = n_{m,k} - n_{m,k-1}$, drawn as independent normal increments:$$\Delta y_{m,k}^{(s)} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\theta_m^{(s)}, \frac{\sigma_m^2}{\Delta n_{m,k}}\right)$$The cumulative mean $y_{m,k}^{(s)}$ is then recursively formed as a sample-size weighted average of the prior cumulative mean and the increment. The sequential Average Type I Error converges to the empirical proportion of simulated trajectories that cross $D_{1,k}(y_{2,k}^{(s)})$ at any stage $k \le K$




