\subsection{Simulation Framework and Setup}
\textbf{Concept:} Describe the computational setup in R (e.g., using \texttt{RBesT} or \texttt{gsbDesign}). State the assumption of normally distributed endpoints. \\
\textit{How to use:} Anchor this setup strictly to the methodology evaluated by Weru et al. for normally distributed endpoints \cite{weru_information_2026}.

\subsection{Application 1: Borrowing on the Control Arm}
\textbf{Concept:} Simulating a hybrid-control trial where historical data informs the placebo/control arm. \\
\textit{How to use:} Vary the tuning parameters (variance, location, weight) of the robust component. Evaluate how $at1e_{seq}$, Assurance, and the \textbf{expected EUII} behave across various prior-data conflict (drift) scenarios, directly mirroring the investigations in Weru et al. but with sequential stopping \cite{weru_information_2026}.

\subsection{Application 2: Borrowing on the Treatment Effect}
\textbf{Concept:} Simulating scenarios where external data directly informs the treatment contrast (e.g., extrapolation). \\
\textit{How to use:} Base this application on the concepts in Psioda \& Xue, showing how adult data is extrapolated to pediatric populations \cite{psioda_bayesian_2020}. Show how $at1e_{seq}$ and EUII react when early stopping is permitted based on this extrapolated information.

